---
title: "Namespaces"
flm:
  use: [flmdoc]
  math: false
---
The families of names the registry holds, each with every name it holds.

\docref{namespaces/feature}{Namespace feature}: A feature is a unit of the registry: it declares commands, stages, tables, settings and the other entities listed here, under names it owns.

\docref{namespaces/package}{Namespace package}: A package is a named bundle of features, together with the packages it builds on, that a document or the program chooses to use.

\docref{namespaces/element}{Namespace element}: An element type says what a node of a document's tree means; the nodes of every command carry an element of one type.

\docref{namespaces/capability}{Namespace capability}: A capability is a named condition that a stage brings about when it runs, such as a table having been written; a plan schedules the stages that provide the capabilities it needs.

\docref{namespaces/table}{Namespace table}: A table holds data that one stage computes over a document and later stages read.

\docref{namespaces/collection}{Namespace collection}: A collection is a name for a set of capabilities: demanding the collection demands every member.

\docref{namespaces/stage}{Namespace stage}: A stage is one step of the processing of a document: it rewrites the trees, computes a table, checks something, or writes output.

\docref{namespaces/lowering-rule}{Namespace lowering-rule}: A lowering rule turns the nodes of one element type into elements that a backend writes natively.

\docref{namespaces/backend}{Namespace backend}: A backend turns a document into one output format, through its lowering, emit and final stages.

\docref{namespaces/output}{Namespace output}: An output is a set of files that a plan can produce, such as an HTML page.

\docref{namespaces/workflow}{Namespace workflow}: A workflow says what a build produces: its outputs, the capabilities it demands, and its preferences among stages and lowering rules.

\docref{namespaces/option-type}{Namespace option-type}: An option type is a typed group of values, set on the registry, on a document or on a render request, that stages and lowering rules read.

\docref{namespaces/service-slot}{Namespace service-slot}: A service slot is a named place where features and the embedding program contribute objects that stages use.

\docref{namespaces/scoped-package}{Namespace scoped-package}: A scoped package is a set of commands defined only inside certain environment bodies or arguments, such as the item command inside a list.

\docref{namespaces/presentation-element}{Namespace presentation-element}: A presentation element is an element of the trees a backend builds from the document's trees; it never appears in a parsed document.

\docref{namespaces/piece}{Namespace piece}: A piece is a value computed over the whole document, such as the list of footnotes, that a backend places before or after the document's content.

\docref{namespaces/style}{Namespace style}: A style definition is a part of a stylesheet that a backend writes into its output when a tree requires it.

\docref{namespaces/script}{Namespace script}: A script definition is a part of a script that a backend writes into its output when a tree requires it.

\docref{namespaces/settings-key}{Namespace settings-key}: A settings key is one value of a feature's settings, written under the feature's name; this list gives each key as the feature's name, a dot and the key.

\docref{namespaces/argument-role}{Namespace argument-role}: An argument role is a registered name for what an argument of a command holds, such as a label or a file path.

